# Copy to .env and edit. Every value here is optional; these are the defaults.

# --- Stock Video Matcher Agent & OmniRoute --------------------------------
OMNIROUTE_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:20128/v1
OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=omniroute
OMNIROUTE_MODEL=agy/gemini-3.7-flash-medium
PEXELS_API_KEY=your_pexels_api_key_here

# Legacy OmniRoute aliases supported by the engine
OMNIROUTE_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:20128/v1
OMNIROUTE_KEY=omniroute

# Leave blank to let the gateway route with auto/* aliases. Pin a model here
# once you know which provider you actually want.
RAHASYA_MODEL_STRONG=agy/claude-sonnet-4-6
RAHASYA_MODEL_CHEAP=agy/gemini-3.7-flash-medium
RAHASYA_MODEL_IMAGE=
RAHASYA_MODEL_EMBED=

# NOTE: provider API keys do NOT go here. They go in OmniRoute's own env file
# at ~/.omniroute/.env, or through its dashboard. See docs/omniroute-setup.md

# Image chain tier 2: a keyless public endpoint, used only when the gateway
# has no image provider. It watermarks and downscales, so it is a bridge, not
# a destination. Set to 0 to skip it and use placeholder frames instead.
RAHASYA_KEYLESS_IMAGES=1

# Optional browser-backed Gemini image provider. Start the Node chatbot server
# and Edge with CDP on port 9222 before rendering. Leave blank to keep the
# existing OmniRoute -> keyless -> placeholder chain unchanged.
RAHASYA_BROWSER_IMAGE_API=http://127.0.0.1:4000/api/image

# --- Voice ---------------------------------------------------------------
# piper (default, local and offline) or edge. Piper was chosen by ear over
# twelve edge-tts voices; it is also MIT-licensed, which retires the open
# question about edge-tts commercial terms.
RAHASYA_VOICE_ENGINE=piper
RAHASYA_PIPER_VOICE=pratham

# Piper reads ~40% faster than edge-tts, so it is slowed a little. This also
# suits the niche. Set 1.0 for its natural pace.
RAHASYA_PIPER_LENGTH=1.12
# Variation in phoneme duration: the knob that most affects whether the
# rhythm ticks like a metronome.
RAHASYA_PIPER_NOISE_W=0.9
RAHASYA_PIPER_NOISE=0.667
RAHASYA_PIPER_SILENCE=0.25

# EQ, de-ess, compression and a small room. Dry synthesis with no space
# around it is a large part of what reads as artificial.
RAHASYA_VOICE_PROCESS=1

# How fast the voice actually speaks, measured. The script agent is given a
# word budget of target_seconds * this, because word count is what decides
# runtime. Re-measure with scripts/measure_speech_rate.py after any voice
# or length_scale change -- but read the number it prints as a ceiling.
#
# 2.29 is what the first fully real run produced: 152 Devanagari words of
# script came out as 66.5s of Piper audio. measure_speech_rate.py says 2.94
# on the same voice, because its five sample lines are clean conversational
# Hindi. Dates, figures and acronyms are much slower per "word" -- Piper
# reads 1965 as "unnees sau painsath", one word and eleven syllables -- and
# real scripts are full of them. 3.03 was set from the sample, and it let
# the agent write a third more words than fit the publishing window.
#
# Whatever you set, it is one number standing in for a spread: 1.83 w/s on
# numeral-heavy lines, 2.94 on clean prose, 2.47 on the sample script. The
# script agent is allowed +/-15% around the budget on top of that, so the
# pre-render length gate accepts the publishing window plus a 15% margin
# rather than the window itself (engine.gates.qc.PRE_RENDER_MARGIN). Do not
# narrow that margin to "tighten" anything: it is what keeps ordinary
# content variance from destroying a run before a human sees a frame.
RAHASYA_WORDS_PER_SEC=2.29

# --- Duration window -------------------------------------------------------
# QC scores the rendered file against a window of target_seconds +/- 15%
# (engine.gates.qc.DURATION_TOLERANCE) -- the same tolerance the script
# agent already allows on word count, carried through to seconds. At the
# shipped target_seconds of 50.0 that is 42.5-57.5s; move the target and
# this window moves with it automatically.
#
# These two only need setting to widen (or narrow) the window independently
# of target_seconds -- e.g. to publish longer or shorter videos than the
# script's own word budget implies. Leave them unset to let the window
# track the target instead.
# RAHASYA_DURATION_MIN=
# RAHASYA_DURATION_MAX=

# edge-tts settings, used when RAHASYA_VOICE_ENGINE=edge.
RAHASYA_VOICE=hi-IN-MadhurNeural
RAHASYA_VOICE_RATE=-8%
RAHASYA_VOICE_PITCH=-6Hz

# --- Grade ---------------------------------------------------------------
# One colour grade over every frame, video clip and fallback still alike.
# A 50s Short pulls 20-26 clips from as many different Pexels creators, all
# shot on different glass in different light; without a shared grade they
# read as a template rather than one video. Set to 0 to render ungraded.
RAHASYA_VIDEO_GRADE=1

# Film grain strength (ffmpeg `noise=alls=`). Split out from the grade
# because it is the most taste-dependent part of the look: a shared noise
# floor is most of what ties mismatched sources together, and it is also
# the first thing anyone wants to retune. 0 keeps the colour, drops the
# grain -- and drops most of the grade's encode cost with it, because the
# grain is what makes the picture expensive to compress.
RAHASYA_VIDEO_GRAIN=9

# --- Emoji stickers ------------------------------------------------------
# A small emoji that pops onto the frame on the exact word that earns it --
# the way retention-focused Shorts editors punctuate a line. The trigger
# vocabulary lives in engine/data/stickers.json, so a new concept is a data
# edit, not a code change. Set to 0 to render without them; off is a
# complete off, and the filtergraph goes back to exactly what it was.
RAHASYA_STICKERS=1

# How many may fire in one video. Restraint is the feature: three over a
# 50s Short is one every ~17 seconds, matching the "3-4 beats only, at the
# biggest moments" rule engine/prompts/script.txt already puts on
# on_screen_text. On top of this there is at most one per beat and 2.5s
# between any two.
RAHASYA_STICKER_MAX=3

# The colour emoji font the sticker PNGs are drawn from, with Pillow and
# embedded_color. Windows ships Segoe UI Emoji; on a Linux VPS point this
# at /usr/share/fonts/truetype/noto/NotoColorEmoji.ttf. If the file is
# missing the render still happens, just without stickers.
RAHASYA_STICKER_FONT=C:\Windows\Fonts\seguiemj.ttf

# Resting size as a fraction of frame width. 0.17 of 1080 is ~184px: reads
# on a phone at arm's length without becoming the shot.
RAHASYA_STICKER_SCALE=0.17

# --- Captions ------------------------------------------------------------
# caption_text = Roman Hinglish (default), voice_text = Devanagari
RAHASYA_CAPTIONS=caption_text
RAHASYA_FONT=Arial
RAHASYA_FONT_DEVA=Nirmala UI

# --- Paths ---------------------------------------------------------------
# RAHASYA_FFMPEG=            # defaults to the imageio-ffmpeg binary
# RAHASYA_WORK=./work
# RAHASYA_OUT=./outputs
# RAHASYA_DB=./engine.db

# --- Guardrail -----------------------------------------------------------
# Pipeline pauses when the day's spend crosses this.
RAHASYA_DAILY_USD=2.0
